<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    THIS PROJECT'S PACKAGE SURFACE IS THE TWO FLUENTVALIDATION ENTRIES BELOW, AND
    NOTHING ELSE. AAP 0.6.1 states it directly: "Application declares only
    FluentValidation". Nothing may be added here on the grounds that a type in this
    layer wants it; a dependency this layer cannot express is a signal about the
    boundary, not a gap in the manifest.

    An earlier revision added Microsoft.Extensions.Options 8.0.2 so a validator
    could take IOptions<PasswordPolicyOptions>. Every technical claim in that
    justification was true - Options really is absent from the Microsoft.NETCore.App
    reference pack, FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions really does not
    supply it transitively, and the generic really does fail to compile without it -
    and none of that made the package approved. The correct resolution was to change
    the consumer rather than the manifest, so the one validator that reached for
    IOptions<T> now takes the bound PasswordPolicyOptions instance directly, exactly
    as its sibling ChangePasswordRequestValidator already did. That is the
    boundary abstraction this layer is meant to use: PasswordPolicyOptions is
    declared here and BOUND BY THE API LAYER, where the web framework supplies the
    options machinery, and the composition root projects the bound instance as a
    resolvable service so the container can hand it to both validators.

    The wider point is that the options PATTERN is not the options PACKAGE. This
    layer participates in the pattern by owning the policy type; only the layer that
    reads configuration needs IOptions<T>, and AAP 0.5.2.2 puts that layer above
    this one. Every IOptions<T> in the backend correctly sits in Api or
    Infrastructure.
  -->
  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      These two entries are the whole of this project's package surface, and the inventory that
      fixes them lists nothing else for this layer. Adding a package here is therefore not a
      local decision: the inventory is frozen, so an entry it does not contain does not belong
      in this file even when a compiler error appears to ask for one.

      That case has already arisen once and is recorded so it is not repeated. IOptions<T> is NOT
      available to this project: FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions 11.12.0 closes
      over FluentValidation and Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions only, so it
      supplies no Options assembly transitively, and Microsoft.Extensions.Options.dll is not in
      the reference pack a Microsoft.NET.Sdk class library targets - it ships in the ASP.NET Core
      shared framework, which this layer must never reference. Compiling a validator whose
      constructor names IOptions<PasswordPolicyOptions> consequently fails with CS0234 and CS0246,
      and that was verified rather than assumed.

      The resolution is to stop asking for IOptions<T>, not to import it. Every validator in
      Validation/ takes the bound options object itself - a plain PasswordPolicyOptions - which is
      a Domain-adjacent Application type this project already owns outright. Nothing is lost: the
      wrapper only defers reading a value that has already been read by the time a validator is
      constructed, and taking the concrete type keeps the constructor honest about needing a
      policy rather than a source of one.

      What this shifts onto the composition root is a real obligation, so it is stated here. The
      Api layer must not only bind the section but also project the bound instance as a resolvable
      concrete service, for example by registering PasswordPolicyOptions from
      IOptions<PasswordPolicyOptions>.Value. If it binds without projecting, every validator in
      this project fails loudly at construction, which is the intended outcome - a missing policy
      registration is a startup defect and must not degrade into a silently unvalidated password.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation" Version="11.12.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="FluentValidation.DependencyInjectionExtensions" Version="11.12.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="..\DnnMigration.Domain\DnnMigration.Domain.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
